## 2. Compound Component Pattern (Reusable UI)

Use for reusable UI components in `src/components/ui/` that have logical sub-parts.

### When to Use

- Component has 2+ related sub-parts that share internal state
- API should be declarative and composable (composition over configuration)

### When NOT to Use

- No sub-parts or no shared internal state
- Purely presentational
- Simpler props API is clearer

### Implementation

- Use Context for shared internal state (never prop-drill)
- Expose sub-components as static properties
- Create `useComponentContext()` hook that throws if used outside provider

```tsx
// List.context.ts
const ListContext = createContext<ListContextType | null>(null)

export const useListContext = () => {
  const ctx = useContext(ListContext)
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('List sub-components must be used within <List>')
  return ctx
}

// List.tsx
const ListRoot = ({ children }: ListProps) => (
  <ListContext.Provider value={value}>
    <ul>{children}</ul>
  </ListContext.Provider>
)

ListRoot.Item = ListItem
ListRoot.Header = ListHeader

export const List = ListRoot
```

### Usage

```tsx
// ✅ Composition (preferred)
<List>
  <List.Header>Users</List.Header>
  <List.Item>John</List.Item>
</List>

// ❌ Configuration props (avoid)
<List items={[]} header="Users" selectable />
```

