## 3. Code Standards

### TypeScript

- Explicit prop types using named `type` or `interface`, exported
- Use `ReactNode` for children
- Use discriminated unions for variants
- No `any`, no `React.FC`

```tsx
// ✅ Correct
type ButtonProps = { label: string; onClick: () => void }
const Button = ({ label, onClick }: ButtonProps) => { ... }

// ❌ Wrong — do not use React.FC
const Button: React.FC<Props> = ({ label, onClick }) => { ... }
```

### Components

- Function components only (no class components)
- Prefer named exports
- No HOCs unless strictly required
- Keep components focused (< 300 lines)

### Imports

- Always use `@/` alias for cross-folder imports (never relative `../../`)
- Order: React & externals → `@/components/ui` → `@/features/*` → `@/types` → `@/services` → Local `./`

### Naming

- Components: `PascalCase.tsx`
- Hooks: `useCamelCase.ts`
- Types: `PascalCase`
- Services: `camelCase`

---

## 4. Anti-Patterns

- ❌ Business logic or API calls in view components
- ❌ Prop drilling shared internal state (use Context for compound components, store for global)
- ❌ Relative imports across folders (`../../`)
- ❌ Monolithic components (> 300 lines)
- ❌ Boolean prop explosions on UI components
- ❌ Using `React.FC` for component signatures
- ❌ Overusing Context for global state (use Zustand store instead)
- ❌ Premature memoization — only memoize when measured
